WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.345
New York, Paris, Tokyo, Moscow, Seoul, Beijing, Delhiㅡmore than 150 cities around the world have subways.

2
00:00:08.346 --> 00:00:11.545
A subway is an electric underground railway.

3
00:00:11.546 --> 00:00:16.418
It makes it easy to get around downtown and connects a city to the suburbs.

4
00:00:16.419 --> 00:00:20.845
The oldest subway, and one of the biggest, is the London Tube.

5
00:00:20.846 --> 00:00:26.316
The London subway is called the Tube because its tunnels are narrow and round like a tube.

6
00:00:26.317 --> 00:00:35.493
In fact, its tunnels are the smallest in the world, but the Tube is one of the longest subways, with 391 kilometers of track.

7
00:00:35.494 --> 00:00:39.305
The Tube was the world’s first underground rail system.

8
00:00:39.306 --> 00:00:46.748
It was built in 1863. But why did the city of London decide to build a railway underground?

9
00:00:46.749 --> 00:00:52.201
Well, in those days, the streets of London were very busy and dirty!

10
00:00:52.202 --> 00:00:58.057
Back then, the cheapest way to get around town was the omnibusㅡa horse-drawn bus.

11
00:00:58.058 --> 00:01:02.808
Twenty people could sit inside an omnibus, but it wasn’t a pleasant experience.

12
00:01:02.809 --> 00:01:06.936
It was crowded, and there was dirty straw on the floor.

13
00:01:06.937 --> 00:01:10.557
People complained a lot about riding in an omnibus.

14
00:01:10.558 --> 00:01:17.323
So in 1836 the London Times newspaper published suggestions to make the journey more pleasant.

15
00:01:17.324 --> 00:01:23.006
They included: 1. Sit up straight and don’t take the space of two people.

16
00:01:23.007 --> 00:01:28.435
2. Do not sit on the straw. You are not in a pigsty.

17
00:01:28.436 --> 00:01:33.339
3. If you have a dog, make sure it is small and has a leash.

18
00:01:33.340 --> 00:01:37.992
4. Do not bring large parcelsㅡan omnibus is not a van.

19
00:01:37.993 --> 00:01:43.616
5. Don’t expect the omnibus conductor to have change; he is not a banker.

20
00:01:43.617 --> 00:01:47.561
6. Don’t act like you are better than other passengers.

21
00:01:47.562 --> 00:01:52.843
If you can afford to ride a private horse and carriage, then do so!

22
00:01:52.844 --> 00:02:00.738
Riding inside the omnibus may have been bad, but stepping out of the bus and onto the London streets was even worse.

23
00:02:00.739 --> 00:02:07.192
With thousands of horses trotting along, the streets were filled withㅡyou guessed itㅡhorse poo!

24
00:02:07.193 --> 00:02:12.541
The horse dung was so bad that people, often children, worked as crossing sweepers.

25
00:02:12.542 --> 00:02:17.261
Their job was to sweep in front of rich people as they crossed the road.

26
00:02:17.262 --> 00:02:22.018
You can imagine that if people could avoid traveling in the streets, they would!

27
00:02:22.019 --> 00:02:26.885
So when the Tube started in the late 1800s, it was a great success!

28
00:02:26.886 --> 00:02:32.393
Back then, the first Tube carriages were long, narrow, and had no windows.

29
00:02:32.394 --> 00:02:38.334
The Tube’s original designers thought there was nothing to see in a tunnel, so why have windows?

30
00:02:38.335 --> 00:02:42.610
By the twentieth century, the Tube had expanded all over London.

31
00:02:42.611 --> 00:02:48.533
During World War I and World War II, Londoners used the Tube stations as bomb shelters.

32
00:02:48.534 --> 00:02:52.564
London was one of the worst-bombed cities during World War II.

33
00:02:52.565 --> 00:02:58.090
People would buy tickets and never come out, because the Tube station was the safest place to be.

34
00:02:58.091 --> 00:03:06.174
From 1939 to 1945, nearly 63 million people stayed in 79 of London’s Tube stations.

35
00:03:06.175 --> 00:03:11.688
Restaurants and libraries were set up underground, and performers entertained people.

36
00:03:11.689 --> 00:03:15.804
Even with modern technology, the Tube still has problems.

37
00:03:15.805 --> 00:03:19.480
There’s always the danger of flooding from the Thames River.

38
00:03:19.481 --> 00:03:29.347
After giant flood gates were built in 1984, flooding greatly decreased, but some Tube stations still get flooded during thunderstorms.

39
00:03:29.348 --> 00:03:36.326
In the summers it is very hot inside a Tube carriage, and air conditioners can’t be installed for two reasons.

40
00:03:36.327 --> 00:03:41.284
First, they give off too much heat, and second, the carriages are too small.

41
00:03:41.285 --> 00:03:46.993
The mayor of London offered a reward of ten thousand pounds for a solution to this problem!

42
00:03:46.994 --> 00:03:50.920
If you ever go to London, make sure you take the Tube!

43
00:03:50.921 --> 00:03:54.975
Look for a round red sign and go down the stairs.

44
00:03:54.976 --> 00:04:03.465
Buy a ticket, wait on the narrow platform, then get on the Tubeㅡand remember you’re sitting inside a part of London’s history!

